WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:06.660
1) Idea Every Day

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:07.660
Scott Myers, DePaul University Professor/Screenwriter/Creator
Gointothestory.blcklst(dot)com: First of all

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:13.710
let’s just image that we have ten track
of our brain, that our brain can do ten things

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:24.289
at once, one of those tracks as a writer should
be sensitive or aware of ideas.

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:27.750
They are out there, they are out there all
of the time all over the place.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:31.929
They are in newspaper articles, they are in
conversations you hear with people in a coffee

00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:33.720
shop, they are on the radio.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.960
They are on click, your click, click, click,
thing that happens.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:46.510
For example I know David Rabinowitz and Charlie
Wachtel who wrote BlacKkKlansman I interview

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:51.550
them and I said how did you guys come up with
it and I think David said well I was trying

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:56.870
to avoid writing and so I was doing the click
thing and I stumbled on this Facebook article

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:01.609
about this guy Ron Stallworth the first black
policeman in Colorado Springs who did this

00:01:01.609 --> 00:01:05.080
thing with The Klan so that is how they discovered
it.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.950
The one thing is just be conscience of it.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:17.850
The other thing is be intentional like everyday
you should be thinking about story ideas.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:25.660
It’s going to vary from person to person
but there have been times in my life where

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:31.660
I have literally said I am going to come up
with an idea today, every day.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:36.759
And a third point to that related is Dr. Linus
Pauling who is the only person to win two

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:41.750
Nobel prizes and he has that saying the best
way to come up with good idea is to come up

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:48.929
with a lot of ideas and so that’s a third
point that you can do is write a lot of ideas.

00:01:48.929 --> 00:01:53.099
Keep a file like I’ve got a Word file stretching
back 20 some odd years with literally hundred

00:01:53.099 --> 00:02:00.610
and hundreds of ideas some of them awful but
you keep working that muscle and then eventually

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:05.110
I think chances are you’re going to surface
something.

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.650
Now then you have to assess the ideas.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:14.370
There are certain questions you can ask like
is this big enough to be a movie, who is the

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:19.880
audience for this, what is the hook, that
central conceit.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:28.220
Like when I sold K-9 I remember going into
an executives office and they said what you

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.390
guys did was so brilliant.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:37.270
You took the buddy action comedy formula (the
police cop) and you put in Rin Tin Tin who

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:41.810
was like the biggest star in Hollywood in
the 1920’s.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:46.230
I learned a very valuable lesson there which
is you always when someone gives you a compliment

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.940
even if you didn’t mean it you go yes, that’s
exactly what I mean.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:56.880
I said yes, that’s exactly what I thinking
because I hadn’t thought that at all.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.710
But at that point in time in 1980 I could
walk into any producer’s office, any bar

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.910
where people where hanging out what are you
working on?

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:10.180
Yeah, I got this idea K-9, loner cop, new
partner, police dog.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:11.680
They would see that.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.620
The hook there is the police dog, nobody had
done that.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:22.850
INCEPTION, being able to go inside someone’s….GROUNDHOG
DAY…that thing…what’s that thing that

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:26.960
is going to get it off from something else?

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:35.040
So those are some key…have one part of your
brain going, be intentional about it, be generating

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.490
a lot of ideas and hope that you will come
up with some really good ones (some strong

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:41.980
ones) and then assess them to make sure that
you feel that it’s big enough and good enough

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:43.360
to be a movie.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.230
2) The 100 Idea Theory

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:49.000
William C. Martell, Author/Screenwriter: My
first step in writing a screenplay is coming

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.110
up basically with 100 ideas and picking the
best idea.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:58.010
I have a thing called the 100 Idea Theory,
it doesn’t have to be 100 ideas but if I

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:02.220
come up with 1 idea, that’s 1 idea (it could
be a terrible idea) but if I come up with

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:07.910
100 ideas or 60 ideas or whatever I can comb
through and find the very best idea and then

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:10.819
I take it to Thunderdome.

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:16.760
I take that idea and go if I write the script
everyone is going to pick it apart.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:18.650
I’m going to pick it apart first.

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:25.540
I want to find all the flaws with whatever
the idea is and get past those flaws and remove

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:27.180
them so that the ideas is great

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:28.760
3) Twenty Ways

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:33.390
Karl Iglesias, Author/Screenwriter: That is
actually the bane of a writer’s existence,

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.810
especially a screenwriter, to come up with
something new.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:42.510
When you think about, I think in the second
edition of 101 Habits: Insider’s Secrets

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:45.440
from Hollywood’s Top Writers, Bill Marsilii
one of the screenwriters there, talks about

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.990
doing the 20’s.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:54.340
So for him anything that he thinks about,
any event, any scene, character whatever,

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:58.250
he thinks of kind of listing 20 different
ways of doing it.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:03.790
He talks about the meet-cute in romantic comedy,
he talks about how people meet in a cute way.

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:06.580
Well he’s going to do 20 different thoughts
about it.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:08.850
“Okay it could be this, it could be this…”

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:14.070
He does 20 because the first two or three
or five of them will be clichés.

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:17.990
They will be things that you’re automatically
going to think about because they’re the

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:20.770
most famous ones that we’ve seen a hundred
times.

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:26.000
But going deeper (and he forces himself to
go deeper) that is where you get the creativity

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.000
and originality.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.590
And eventually when you reach past the 10
to 12 or 15 all the way up to 20, you’ll

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:36.780
start getting really original ways because
you force yourself.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.050
And so that is what the writer needs to do.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:43.900
Force yourself to go deeper in order to create
some original writing.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:45.880
Do the 20 for just anything.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:48.770
It could be 20 different ways to rob a bank.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:52.390
20 different ways to say I love you.

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:54.389
20 different ways to break up with someone.

00:05:54.389 --> 00:05:56.250
20 different ways to kill someone.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.320
There are all these different things that
you’ve seen a 100 times and when you see

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:04.380
it over and over again you go “Hmm…”
because you’ve seen it a 100 times and it’s

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:08.850
only the movies that break out…stories that
break out are the ones that are truly original,

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:09.850
right?

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:11.360
4) What’s The Big Problem?

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:17.040
Erik Bork, Author/Screenwriter: To me where
it starts is a really compelling problem that

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.910
is going to take the whole story to be solved.

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:24.830
That millions of strangers consuming that
story would be able to identify with this

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:30.770
problem and care that it gets solved and be
really entertained by the process of watching

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:32.960
people trying to solve it.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:37.310
So that’s kind of a lot of things in one
but to me that’s kind of where it all starts

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:38.310
with.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:43.560
There is something that someone is going through
in life that really matters to them and hopefully

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:44.620
to us as the audience.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:47.080
It’s really important, it’s really difficult.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:55.000
It’s going to take a lot of effort and active
action toward trying to solve it which isn’t

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:56.430
going to work.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:01.410
So it’s difficult, it’s complicated, trying
to solve it usually leads to pushback and

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:05.820
complications and things that don’t go as
planned and so that tends to build that sense

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:11.080
of problematic situations so it’s growing
and escalating, building over the course of

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:16.480
the story and then in the end there is some
kind of resolution that has taken the audience

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:21.560
on a journey and usually taken certain characters
on a journey where they’re not the same

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:27.000
as what they were and therefore it was meaningful
and important in some way that they went through

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.000
that.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:29.000
5) True Life Stories

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:35.400
Stanley M. Brooks, Producer/Director/Screenwriter:
There is certainly the wow factor of that

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:37.460
is the most amazing thing I’ve ever heard.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:38.460
I have to have that.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:39.500
I have to have that right now.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:44.960
How do I get that story, that book or magazine
or true life story.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:50.139
When I heard the story of Christine Pelisek
a fact-checker at 23-years-old and working

00:07:50.139 --> 00:07:55.560
at LA Weekly and breaking the Grim Sleeper
serial killer in Los Angeles I didn’t need

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:57.130
to know anymore.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:01.139
But then there are stories that I just can’t
let go of and I will go back to and go back

00:08:01.139 --> 00:08:06.380
to and those are the ones hopefully the next
movie I’m going to make in the Amazon was

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:12.540
a story that I chased for five years, it’s
a true life story and I just couldn’t let

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:13.670
it go.

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:18.070
Some other producer got it and failed at developing
it.

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:22.520
I would call every 6 months and after about
a year and a half they abandoned the other

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:27.680
producer and went with me and I just could
not let it go.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:32.159
It was a story I had to tell one way or another
I was going to tell that story, that is the

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:39.319
true story of Juliane Koepcke who in 1972
was living in Peru going to The American School.

00:08:39.319 --> 00:08:42.909
She was 18 and in her senior year.

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:44.370
Her parents were noted Zoologists.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:47.480
She spent part of her childhood in the Amazon.

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:51.589
Her parents were cataloging butterflies and
bats and then when she got to middle school

00:08:51.589 --> 00:08:59.260
they moved her to Lima and she spoke perfect
German, perfect English, perfect Spanish.

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:03.430
Because they are on a different schedule than
us because there seasons are different than

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:07.029
us the school year ends in December.

00:09:07.029 --> 00:09:13.550
So she was both graduating and going to her
prom in December and her dad wanted them to

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:22.929
go to the Amazon on Christmas Eve and she
wanted to stay and so he went early and she

00:09:22.929 --> 00:09:28.639
and her mom stayed behind in Lima and she
went to her prom and graduated with her friends

00:09:28.639 --> 00:09:33.670
and then they got on a plane and when the
plane was over the Amazon it got hit by lightening

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:41.089
twice and it disintegrated in mid air and
she was in the window seat strapped in and

00:09:41.089 --> 00:09:47.070
as she said I didn’t leave the plane, the
plane left me and she found herself 15,000

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:57.009
feet in her chair upside down pinwheeling
and she blacked out, fell, hit the canopy

00:09:57.009 --> 00:09:59.860
of the Amazon which broke her fall (it’s
a miracle).

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:07.370
She is the longest fall without a parachute,
and the canopy broke her fall and she landed

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:11.360
in the Amazon, woke up, had nothing but a
broken collar bone that she knew of.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:18.140
She had one shoe, lost her glasses and for
the next 9 days followed the river and the

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:26.819
lessons that she learned from her father and
her own desire not to perish and was rescued.

00:10:26.819 --> 00:10:30.809
She is a friend of mine now (she is now in
her 50’s), she’s an extraordinary woman,

00:10:30.809 --> 00:10:35.120
she spends half of the year in Munich and
the other half in the Amazon trying to save

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:36.120
the rainforest.

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:43.779
And we are going to go down to Bogota and
the Amazon and hopefully tell that story some

00:10:43.779 --> 00:10:44.970
time next year.

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:46.170
6) What If?

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:52.550
Brian Avenet-Bradley, Filmmaker: I think for
me a movie really begins with an idea or the

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:59.529
key story element or the what if.

00:10:59.529 --> 00:11:03.930
Some times Lo [filmmaking partner Laurence
Avenet-Bradley] will have an idea or a dream

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:06.439
and say This is really great.

00:11:06.439 --> 00:11:13.189
And I always jot it down, her dream and ideas
and my dreams, when I have an idea or thought

00:11:13.189 --> 00:11:17.050
I wake up from or if I am daydreaming.

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:22.920
I try to write all of those little thoughts
down and then eventually what I find happens

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:29.139
is some of those that get written down there
is one that kind of keeps chirping, keeps

00:11:29.139 --> 00:11:30.139
like Hey!

00:11:30.139 --> 00:11:32.709
More ideas come to it and at a certain point
it reaches critical mass.

00:11:32.709 --> 00:11:39.129
Usually when I have 10 or 12 pages of ideas
relating to a particular story it kind of

00:11:39.129 --> 00:11:43.759
reaches the critical mass where I start going
okay now I’m going to figure out what this

00:11:43.759 --> 00:11:46.009
is as a real script.

00:11:46.009 --> 00:11:49.690
All of these ideas are great and then figuring
how it all coalesces into the script.

00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:55.689
And then it kind of evolves like I work with
Lo when I finish a first draft because she

00:11:55.689 --> 00:11:58.129
usually gets fresh eyes on it at that point.

00:11:58.129 --> 00:12:03.600
So it’s great to get a fresh perspective
and then the story grows and of course as

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:10.779
you are inspired by locations or real things
you experience and oftentimes as it progresses

00:12:10.779 --> 00:12:17.329
like when you start casting an actor can be
a huge influence in terms of like oh this

00:12:17.329 --> 00:12:19.009
person changes that character.

00:12:19.009 --> 00:12:20.699
This could make the movie turn into this.

00:12:20.699 --> 00:12:25.040
So all of those things and it just kind of
grows so it’s fun.

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:26.879
It’s probably my favorite part.

00:12:26.879 --> 00:12:28.730
7) Movie scenes and dialogue

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:36.829
Ryan Harris, Filmmaker: A lot of them just
stem from stories that I would love to see

00:12:36.829 --> 00:12:41.929
as a filmmaker, that I would love to go to
the movies and watch and a lot of it comes

00:12:41.929 --> 00:12:48.199
from movies that I’ve watched over and over
and over again or a lot of the inspiration

00:12:48.199 --> 00:12:52.119
comes from movies in general.

00:12:52.119 --> 00:12:55.589
You watch a scene and you think to yourself
that would be really interesting to…even

00:12:55.589 --> 00:12:59.980
a line of dialogue that the character could
say you take that and you go that in and of

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:01.290
itself could be a story.

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:04.929
That is something that would be interesting
to explore so we just try to find things that

00:13:04.929 --> 00:13:12.100
we would love to see as filmmakers and writers
and stories that we would love to be told.

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:18.319
There is actually a really interesting old
Canadian movie.

00:13:18.319 --> 00:13:25.239
I say old, the late 90’s at it was called
When Good Ghouls Go Bad and it stars Christopher

00:13:25.239 --> 00:13:29.089
Lloyd who is one of the most amazing actors.

00:13:29.089 --> 00:13:34.410
Everybody knows him as Doc Brown in BACK TO
THE FUTURE but a lot of his stuff is serious

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:39.230
work and his comedic work is just fantastic.

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:44.809
So he has a line where he is speaking to his
son and his son basically says you know you

00:13:44.809 --> 00:13:52.379
used to be this go-getter and the town is
dealing with passing of this person who got

00:13:52.379 --> 00:13:59.699
stuck in this kiln overnight and the town
sort of deals with that heavy weight and the

00:13:59.699 --> 00:14:04.970
dad basically says you know you used to be
happy, you used to be a go-getter, you used

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:11.129
to build things and make things and help people
and Christoper Lloyd says yeah and I built

00:14:11.129 --> 00:14:14.079
the kiln that that person died in.

00:14:14.079 --> 00:14:18.720
It’s one of those moments in that movie
where he didn’t need to say that, that didn’t

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:24.371
need to be a part of the movie, but it was
and that right there you go man that in and

00:14:24.371 --> 00:14:31.100
of itself dealing with the trauma of doing
something you love and then that thing you

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:35.509
love hurts somebody, that in and of itself
could be an incredible story.

00:14:35.509 --> 00:14:40.259
There are a lot of things in movies and I
try my best to write them down when I feel

00:14:40.259 --> 00:14:45.209
them and see them and I hear them but that
would definitely be an example of something

00:14:45.209 --> 00:14:46.910
that has inspired me.

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:48.819
8) The Comp Game

00:14:48.819 --> 00:14:53.480
Travis Seppala, Author/Screenwriter: You want
to come up with an idea for a story but you

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:55.459
don’t know really where to start.

00:14:55.459 --> 00:15:09.920
One good place to start 

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:30.579
that I 
have 

00:15:30.579 --> 00:15:55.889
in my book is the 

00:15:55.889 --> 00:15:57.449
comp game.

00:15:57.449 --> 00:16:31.999
For those 

00:16:31.999 --> 00:17:09.610
of 
you 

00:17:09.610 --> 00:18:23.970
who don’t know what 
a 

00:18:23.970 --> 00:19:01.670
comp 
is ‘This-meets-that.’

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:03.860
A movie like HARRY-MEETS-SALLY-meets-AIRBUD.

00:19:03.860 --> 00:19:15.390
What is 
that movie going to be?

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:28.650
So there is your assignment.

00:19:28.650 --> 00:19:42.650
You come up 

00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:42.830
with

